# The Agent CLI Contract, as used by `{{ .Tool }}`

Everything on this page is the same for every tool built to Agent CLI Contract {{ .Contract }}. Read
it once and it applies to all of them. For this tool specifically, run `{{ .Tool }} teach`.

## Calling a tool

- One call does one thing and prints **one JSON document** to stdout, including on failure.
- **Check the exit code first.** It tells you what happened before you read anything.
- Commands have dotted names that map onto the command line: `{{ .Example.Name }}` is
  `{{ .Tool }} {{ .Example.Argv }}`.
- Nothing is interactive. There are no prompts, colours, or progress output.

## Reading the response

```json
{"ok":true,"tool":"{{ .Tool }}","command":"{{ .Example.Name }}","data":[…],"meta":{"page":{…},"contract_version":"{{ .Contract }}","tool_version":"{{ .ToolVersion }}"}}
```

- `ok` says whether it worked. Branch on it.
- `data` is an array for lists, an object for one item, `null` on failure.
- `meta.page` tells you where you are in a list (see Paging).
- `meta.warnings` lists things worth knowing that are not failures. Read it.
- `meta.window` shows the time range actually searched. If `source` is `default`, the tool chose it,
  and older results may exist.

An empty list is a success, not "not found".

## When it fails

```json
{"ok":false,…,"error":{"code":"not_found","exit_code":5,"message":"…","retriable":false,"hint":"…"}}
```

`hint` is usually a command you can run as-is. `retriable` means trying again later may work; it
does not mean retry immediately.

| Exit | Code | What to do |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `internal` | A bug in the tool. Stop and report it |
| 2 | `usage` | Your command line is wrong. Read `hint` and `details.did_you_mean` |
| 3 | `config` | The tool is misconfigured. The operator must fix it; run `{{ .Tool }} doctor` to see what |
| 4 | `auth` | A credential is missing or rejected. The operator must fix it; run `{{ .Tool }} doctor` |
| 5 | `not_found` | The thing does not exist, or you cannot see it |
| 6 | `validation` | Upstream rejected what you sent. Fix the values |
| 7 | `conflict` | It changed underneath you. Read it again, then decide |
| 8 | `refused` | The tool declined: usually a write without `--confirm`. `details.reason` says why |
| 9 | `rate_limited` | Wait `retry_after_ms`, then retry |
| 10 | `timeout` | Retry with a smaller request. For a write, check first whether it happened |
| 11 | `network` | Could not reach upstream. Run `{{ .Tool }} doctor` |
| 12 | `upstream` | Upstream failed. Retry later |
| 13 | `partial` | You got some of the data; `meta.errors` says what is missing |
| 14 | `cache_miss` | A stored dataset expired. Re-run the query in `hint` |
| 130, 143 | `canceled` | The call was interrupted |

Codes 32–63 are specific to a tool and listed by `describe`.

## Finding your way around

| Question | Command |
|---|---|
| What can this tool do? | `tools` (names only, cheapest) |
| What does each command do? | `tools --detail` |
| How exactly do I call one? | `describe <name>` |
| How do I use this tool well? | `teach`, then `teach --list` |
| Is it set up correctly? | `doctor` |
| What is it configured as, and why? | `list-config` |
| Which profiles can it run with? | `list-profiles` |

Go only as deep as you need. `describe` with no name returns every schema, which is large.

## Getting less output

Output costs context, so every tool starts small and lets you ask for more.

| Control | Default | Use it to |
|---|---|---|
| `--fields a,b,c` | a short default set | Get only the fields you need, in that order |
| `--fields -desc` | | Drop fields from the default set |
| `--fields '*'` | | Get everything (large) |
| `--limit N` | 25 | Get more or fewer records |
| `--since`, `--until` | last 24 hours | Search a different time range |
| `--max-bytes`, `--max-string` | 32 KB, 2048 chars | Allow larger output when you really need it |
| `--keep-empty` | empty values removed | See fields that are empty rather than absent |

Time formats: `-2h`, `-7d`, `2026-09-30`, `2026-09-30T14:00:00+02:00`, epoch seconds or
milliseconds, and `now` for `--until`. A date-time without a timezone is rejected on purpose.

If a value was cut short, its path is in `meta.elided_fields`.

## Paging

The tool pages through upstream for you: ask for `--limit 60` and you get 60 in one call.

```json
"page":{"limit":60,"count":60,"total":214,"total_is_exact":true,"has_more":true,"next_cursor":"…","truncated":false}
```

To continue, pass `next_cursor` back **on its own**:

```
{{ .Tool }} {{ .Example.Argv }} --cursor <next_cursor>
```

The cursor already carries your original parameters. Stop when `has_more` is `false`.

If `truncated` is `true`, the tool hit a size or page cap before reaching your limit;
`truncated_reason` says which. You lose nothing: `next_cursor` resumes exactly where it stopped.
{{ if .HasDatasets }}
## Getting everything: datasets

When you know you want the whole result set, add `--all`. The tool fetches all of it once, in as few
upstream calls as it can, stores it on disk, and returns the first part plus a handle:

```json
"dataset":{"id":"…","path":"/…/datasets/….jsonl","record_count":4213,"complete":true,"ttl_seconds":3600,"read_hint":"{{ .Tool }} dataset read … --offset 25 --limit 100"}
```

Then any of:

- `{{ .Tool }} dataset read <id> --cursor <next_cursor>` — the next part, no upstream calls.
- `{{ .Tool }} dataset read <id> --offset 2000 --limit 50` — any slice.
- Read `path` directly: it is JSON Lines, one record per line, already trimmed to your `--fields`.

`dataset list`, `dataset stat <id>`, `dataset rm <id>`, and `dataset clear --confirm` manage them.

A dataset expires `ttl_seconds` after you last read it. If `complete` is `false`, a cap stopped the
collection early and it is not the whole set.
{{ end }}
## Writes

{{- if .WritesEnabled }}

Commands that change upstream are marked `"mutates":true` in `tools --detail`, for example
`{{ .Tool }} {{ .WriteExample.Argv }}`.

- Without `--confirm`, a write is **refused** and shows you exactly the request it would have sent.
- `--dry-run` shows the request without sending it, on any command.
- A write that times out is never retried automatically. Check whether it happened before trying
  again.
{{- else }}

This build is **read-only**. Commands that change upstream are not available.
{{- end }}

## Credentials and configuration

- Credentials live in the tool's environment, never in your commands. The tool refuses
  `--api-key`, `--token`, and similar flags, and treats any key passed that way as leaked.
- You never need to see a credential. `doctor` tells you whether it works; `list-config` tells you
  where it comes from.
- Settings come, in order of priority, from: a flag; `{{ .Prefix }}_<SETTING>_<PROFILE>`;
  `{{ .Prefix }}_<SETTING>`; the config file's active profile; the config file's `default`
  section; `AGENTCLI_<SETTING>`; the built-in default.
- `--profile <name>` changes only the settings that profile defines. Everything else stays as it was.

`{{ .Tool }} teach config` covers all of it in detail, including an example config file.

## Human-readable output

Add `--human` to any command for a table or plain text instead of JSON. It is meant for a person
reading a terminal; do not parse it. The exit code is the same either way.
